node_modules
build
dist
.cache
.output
.wxt
# GitHub Pages staging directory (see .github/workflows/static.yml)
_site/
*.local
# Local-only credential file. Nothing in this repository reads it any more — `release.yml` takes its
# Chrome Web Store credentials from repository Secrets and calls the upload/publish API with `curl` —
# but a file by this name is exactly the shape a `wxt-publish-extension`-style tool writes, so the
# ignore stays as the cheap guard against pasting an extension ID and refresh token into a public repo.
.env.submit
.test-files
.test-screenshots
# Measurement scratch: baseline logs, size snapshots, one-off CDP probe output. Same category as the
# two above — a per-machine work area, whose contents describe one worktree at one moment. Nothing
# in `scripts/` or `.github/workflows/` reads this path, so ignoring it costs no verification step.
# It does need the two companions this line cannot supply: `.test-tmp/` is in `eslint.config.mjs`'s
# global ignores and in `.prettierignore`, because both tools walk the filesystem rather than git.
.test-tmp/
.tmp-e2e-profile
# Partial-staging scratch (`git apply --cached` patches plus the 33-line helper that builds them).
# A per-machine work area like the three above: the 18 `.diff` files are byte snapshots of one
# worktree at one moment. They reached `main` in `b441af2`, and the helper broke `pnpm lint` there —
# `process` and `console` are not globals under this ESLint config — which took `lint:all` and CI's
# lint job down with it. Note `.test-screenshots` is gitignored yet still linted: ESLint walks the
# filesystem, not git, so a probe left there fails the gate just the same.
.tmp-staging
# A one-off verification screenshot from an early build, tracked since the first commit. This
# extension has no popup at all (`entrypoints/` is `background.ts` + `options/`, and `AGENTS.md` says
# so), so the file documented a surface that never shipped; `.qoder/specs/2026-09-19-…-design.md` had
# already recorded it as contradicting that claim. Nothing references it as an asset — no README, no
# `docs/`, no script — so removing it from the index cannot break a link, only a history view.
popup-verify.png
# Generated paste-ready drafts (images inlined as data URIs, ~MBs each); the .md sources are tracked.
docs/promo/*.html
.DS_Store
# Qoder agent local settings (per-machine approvals); `.qoder/rules/` stays tracked
.qoder/settings.local.json
# Working notes, not repo deliverables: per-round design specs, audit reports and agent execution
# plans. They deliberately live here rather than in `docs/`, because `docs/` is the GitHub Pages
# source root (`static.yml` publishes everything in it except `promo/`) — see `.qoder/rules/wxt-rules.md`
# §10. Nothing builds on them: no script or workflow opens these paths. `verify:numbers` checks a
# hardcoded prose list (`docs/**`, `CHROMEWEBSTORE.md`, the two `.github/` runbooks, both CHANGELOGs)
# that never names `.qoder/`, and `release.mjs` puts `:(exclude).qoder/**` on the commit range it
# summarizes. The only mention from outside this directory is the historical note about
# `popup-verify.png` further up in this file, which reads the same either way.
# `.qoder/rules/` is NOT in this group — that directory is the project's coding contract and must
# stay tracked, so do not "tidy" these two lines into a blanket `.qoder/`. And note that
# `.github/visibility-checklist.md` / `CWS_PUBLISHING_GUIDE*.md` are in `verify:numbers`' list: those
# operational docs are tracked on purpose and untracking them turns CI red.
.qoder/specs/
.qoder/plans/
# What the two rules above do NOT do: `.gitignore` cannot untrack. The specs and plans that were
# committed before this rule existed stay on the remote until someone runs
# `git rm --cached` on them (local copies survive; history does not get rewritten).
